      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Andy Montroll would have defeated either rival in a two-way race, but IRV\'s sequential eliminations elected Bob Kiss. The case is the standard demonstration that eliminating "spoilers" by transferring votes does not eliminate all paradoxes — it moves them.',
    },
    {
      q: 'The 1993 New Zealand referendum switched the country from FPTP to MMP. Why do political scientists treat it as a showcase of referendum practice?',
      choices: [
        'It was decided by parliament instead of voters',
        'It was a clear constitutional question, run with a two-stage process and independent commission designing the new system — the design features referendums need',
        'It was held during a war',
