---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/intermediate/voting-systems-and-electoral-design">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate political science lesson. It assumes the foundational lesson
    <em>Forms of Government</em> — you should already know what an election is
    <em>for</em> in a democracy and how executive power sits in different regimes — and
    comfort with percentages and simple indexes (the analysis section uses no more
    arithmetic than that). The sibling lesson <em>Constitutions and Rights</em> pairs
    well with the referendum section.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain how first-past-the-post translates votes into seats, and why Duverger's law follows;</li>
    <li>compare the proportional families — party list, STV, MMP — and the majoritarian hybrids on their trade-offs;</li>
    <li>state Arrow's impossibility theorem and the Gibbard–Satterthwaite result in plain language;</li>
    <li>evaluate referendum design: question framing, thresholds, sequencing, minority safeguards;</li>
    <li>use the standard measurement tools — disproportionality indexes, the efficiency gap, ensemble simulations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Electoral systems are <strong>translation rules between votes and power</strong> — and
    the rule chosen shapes the party system, the campaigns, the coalitions and the
    outcomes, usually more than voters realise. The syllabus asks three questions: what
    different systems exist, how referendums are used, and how systems are analysed —
    and the analysis lands somewhere genuinely mathematical: fairness criteria provably
    conflict (Arrow), so every democracy is running a chosen compromise rather than the
    correct answer. Learning the systems therefore means learning what each trade-off
    buys and what it costs.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the main systems and the theorem that haunts them all" widget="equationStep" config={voteCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the first three cards are competing translation
    rules — plurality, proportionality, ranked preference — and each card's note quietly
    names what the rule sacrifices. Card 4 is not a system but a theorem, and it
    reframes the whole deck: no arrangement of the first three can satisfy every
    reasonable fairness condition at once. Read card 4 last, with the sections below in
    mind.
  </p>

  <h2>First-past-the-post: the mechanical heart of disproportionality</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Plurality in single-member districts</strong> (the UK, US, Canada, India)
    awards each district to whoever gets the most votes, majority not required. Its
    virtues are real: simplicity (one X), decisiveness, a local member, and —
    usually — a clear winner with a working majority. Its costs are mechanical.
    Votes for losers are <strong>wasted</strong> (they elect nothing), so the conversion
    of votes to seats depends on geography: the UK 2015 election is the canonical
    worked example — UKIP's 12.6% of the national vote (3.9 million votes) returned
    one seat, while the SNP's 4.7% returned 56, because concentration converts and
    dispersion does not. The 2019 election gave the Conservatives a 56%-seat
    majority on 43.6% of the vote; the UK's typical disproportionality index
    (Gallagher's least-squares measure) runs above 20, against low single digits for
    the most proportional systems. The spoiler problem follows: under plurality,
    voting for a third party whose support resembles a major party's can help the
    voter's <em>least</em> preferred candidate (Florida 2000: Bush's 537-vote margin
    against Ralph Nader's 97,000 Florida votes is the permanent cautionary tale).
    The systemic consequence is <strong>Duverger's law</strong> (1954): plurality-plus-
    single-member-districts tends toward two parties — a <em>mechanical</em> effect
    (small parties win few seats) compounding a <em>psychological</em> one (voters
    desert parties that cannot win, desertion that becomes self-fulfilling).
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="mark the UK's typical disproportionality score" widget="place" config={vsGallagherPlace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the candidate with the most votes enjoys majority
  support." Plurality requires no majority: with three or more candidates, the winner
  can be the active second choice of most of the electorate — the constituency's votes
  simply split. This is why "won the most votes" and "most voters wanted this winner"
  are different claims, and why majoritarian systems manufacture the majority that
  plurality merely presumes.</p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="bet on what 'most votes' actually means" widget="predict" config={vsPluralityPredict} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> plurality is simple and decisive, and pays for it with
    wasted votes, spoilers and two-party gravity. The obvious repair — make seats match
    votes — brings its own trade-offs.
  </p>

  <h2>Proportional representation and the hybrids</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Party-list PR</strong> fills multi-member districts (or one national
    district — the Netherlands, Israel) so seats match vote shares; the design dial
    is <em>district magnitude</em> (bigger = more proportional) and the
    <em>threshold</em> (Germany's 5% bar keeps extremists and splinters out while
    preserving broad proportionality). List systems deliver the fairest translation
    and the most multiparty politics — with the traded costs: coalitions are normal
    (governance by negotiation), and <strong>closed lists</strong> hand parties
    control of who actually sits (the Netherlands and Israel sit at the
    hyper-proportional end; Poland and others use open lists to give voters candidate
    choice). <strong>Single transferable vote (STV)</strong> — Ireland, Malta, the
    Australian Senate — makes proportionality from candidate-ranking within
    multi-member seats (surplus votes transfer at a fractional value, per the Droop
    quota), keeping local representation AND proportionality, at the price of a
    counting process only specialists love. <strong>Mixed-member proportional
    (MMP)</strong> — Germany since 1949, New Zealand since 1996 — runs the two logics
    side by side: constituency members for place, list members to top the totals up
    to proportionality. <strong>Ranked-choice / alternative vote (IRV)</strong> and
    two-round runoffs (France) are <em>majoritarian</em>, not proportional: they
    manufacture majority legitimacy in single-member seats. IRV's promise is spoiler-
    proofing (your backup rankings transfer when your favourite is eliminated); its
    pathologies are real — Burlington, Vermont's 2009 mayoral race elected a candidate
    who would have lost to either rival head-to-head (the <strong>Condorcet
    winner</strong> — the one who beats everyone pairwise — lost), the textbook case
    that transferring votes moves paradoxes without abolishing them.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file each system feature under its family" widget="sort-buckets" config={vsPRSort} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> proportional representation means instability, and
  FPTP means stability. The comparative record is messier: PR countries (Germany,
  New Zealand, the Nordics) are among the world's most stable and best-governed;
  FPTP countries have produced landslides on a minority of votes, parties governing
  alone on a third of the electorate, and two-party dominance that occasionally
  shatters (Canada, the UK's 2010s). What the system buys is not stability per se
  but a particular <em>kind</em> of stability — decisive single-party government —
  purchased with disproportional translation.</p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drag the divider — two kinds of stability" widget="compare" config={vsStabilityCompare} />

  <h2>Referendums: direct democracy, used with design care</h2>
  <p>
    A referendum asks voters to decide an issue directly. The <strong>uses</strong> are
    patterned: constitutional fundamentals (Scottish independence 2014; Brexit 2016 —
    52-48 on 72% turnout), moral questions that cut across party lines (abortion,
    same-sex marriage ballots), and — the most constructive genre — <strong>electoral
    redesign</strong>: New Zealand's 1992-93 sequence (an indicative vote on preferred
    systems, a binding referendum adopting MMP, then an independent commission drawing
    boundaries) is the showcase because it insulated rule-changing from incumbent
    self-interest. The <strong>pathologies</strong> are equally patterned. Binary
    framing compresses multi-dimensional questions into one lever (the Brexit
    problem: sovereignty, migration, economics and identity in one X); campaigns
    reward emotional framing over deliberation; minority rights face majoritarian
    override (hence constitutional limits — courts voiding referendums that violate
    rights); turnout selects; and <strong>serial referendums</strong> invite the
    "re-run until the right answer" critique (whether Brexit itself counts is the
    live debate — the counter-rule: some questions, once answered, legitimately
    require supermajorities to reopen). The design questions the syllabus wants:
    which decisions belong to voters directly (constitutional identity questions,
    moral cross-cutting issues) versus representatives who can deliberate and amend;
    what thresholds and turnout floors apply (constitutional change on a 51-49
    split of 40% turnout is a fragile mandate); and who frames the question —
    wording effects are measurable and, on close votes, decisive.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="walk the referendum-design decisions in order" widget="branch" config={vsReferendumBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the systems translate votes into power, and referendums
    hand voters a single lever on a single question. The remaining question is the
    analyst's: when the fairness criteria provably conflict, how do you score any of
    these rules at all?
  </p>

  <h2>Electoral system analysis: the criteria, the theorems, the map-drawing</h2>
  <p>
    Analysis means scoring systems against explicit criteria, and the criteria
    conflict. Proportionality against decisiveness; local representation against
    party-list control; simplicity against expressiveness; minority representation
    against fragmentation; resistance to gerrymandering against districting itself.
    The two theorems explain why no scoring wins outright. <strong>Arrow's theorem</strong>
    (1951): with three or more options, no rank-order voting rule can satisfy
    unrestricted domain, the Pareto condition, <strong>independence of irrelevant
    alternatives</strong> (the A-vs-B ranking should not flip because C entered)
    and non-dictatorship simultaneously. IIA's violation is the plurality spoiler
    effect generalised; the Condorcet paradox (majorities can cycle: A beats B
    beats C beats A) is why. <strong>Gibbard–Satterthwaite</strong> (1973/75) adds
    the tactical layer: every non-dictatorial rule with 3+ options is
    <em>manipulable</em> — some situation exists where voting insincerely helps you.
    These are not opinions; they are proofs, and they reframe the whole subject:
    electoral design is engineering under an impossibility result — you choose which
    unfairness to carry. The practical analysis tools: the Gallagher index for
    disproportionality, turnout and validity statistics, strategic-voting rates,
    and — for districted systems — the <strong>gerrymandering</strong> measurements.
    Drawing district lines to predetermine outcomes is as old as the word
    (Elbridge Gerry's 1812 salamander); the modern detection is statistical. The
    <strong>efficiency gap</strong> (Stephanopoulos & McGhee, 2015) measures
    wasted-vote asymmetry — parties whose votes pile up in lost districts and
    safe wins are being cracked and packed. <strong>Ensemble methods</strong>
    simulate thousands of neutral districting plans and ask whether the actual
    map is an outlier. The case study that taught a generation: Pennsylvania 2012,
    where Democratic House candidates won roughly half the statewide vote and
    five of eighteen seats — a map outcome no neutral ensemble would plausibly
    produce. The courts' struggle with these metrics (rejected by the US Supreme
    Court in 2019 as beyond judicial management, adopted in state constitutions)
    is the live frontier of the analysis tradition.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="one line of this gerrymandering briefing is wrong" widget="spot-error" config={vsGerrySpot} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "gerrymandering means districts with weird shapes."
  Shape is the folk detector, and a poor one: natural geography contorts districts too,
  and a compact map can still be rigged. The modern detection is statistical — wasted
  votes packed and cracked asymmetrically (the efficiency gap), or a map that stands
  out against thousands of neutral simulations — because the offence is in the
  <em>outcomes</em>, not the outline.</p>

  <h2>Design choices beyond the ballot</h2>
  <p>
    The wider design layer: <strong>compulsory voting</strong> (Australia since 1924:
    turnout near 90%, versus voluntary systems' 50-65% — and the composition effects
    are the point: who turns out decides elections, so every turnout rule is a
    distributional choice about whose preferences count); <strong>malapportionment</strong>
    (the US Senate gives Wyoming's 580,000 people the same representation as
    California's 39 million — an extreme design choice voters never chose);
    <strong>registration and administration</strong> (US registration burdens depress
    turnout measurably; automatic registration and independent boundary commissions
    are the standard upgrades); and thresholds, terms and sequencing. Each dial is
    independently settable, and combinations — not single choices — produce each
    country's distinctive electoral character.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="bet the magnitude — compulsory turnout" widget="estimate" config={vsTurnoutEstimate} />

  <h2>The ballot itself: design reaches the last centimetre</h2>
  <p>
    The systems logic extends all the way to the paper. The <strong>Florida 2000
    butterfly ballot</strong> is the canonical case: Palm Beach County's two-column
    layout put holes for adjacent entries, and tens of thousands of voters
    mispunched — Patrick Buchanan's tally in that heavily Democratic county ran
    roughly 3,000 votes above his plausible ceiling, in an election decided
    nationally by 537. Ballot design is not decoration; on close margins it is
    the outcome. The same applies to the subtler dials: <strong>candidate order
    effects</strong> (first position carries a measurable few-percentage-point
    advantage, large enough to decide low-information contests — which is why
    some jurisdictions randomise or rotate), <strong>ballot length and
    roll-off</strong> (voters abandon down-ballot races as lists lengthen),
    <strong>overvotes and spoilt ballots</strong> (marking two candidates voids
    the vote — punch-card systems' "hanging chads" were a validity crisis, and
    the post-2002 Help America Vote Act's equipment standards are the reform
    trail), and <strong>question wording</strong> on referendum papers, where
    double negatives and framing shift measurable points of support. The
    general principle for the analysis toolkit: every interface between voter
    and count — district line, threshold, ranking rule, hole punch — is a
    parameter, and on close margins each parameter is a potential kingmaker.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="rebuild the butterfly-ballot failure chain" widget="order" config={vsButterflyOrder} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In the UK 2015 election, UKIP won 12.6% of the national vote and 1 of 650 seats, while the SNP won 4.7% and 56 seats. What mechanical feature of first-past-the-post explains this?</li>
    <li>In Burlington, Vermont's 2009 mayoral race, the "Condorcet winner" lost under instant-runoff voting. What is a Condorcet winner?</li>
    <li>Why is New Zealand's 1992-93 referendum sequence treated as a showcase of referendum design?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) FPTP awards seats per district, so geographically
    concentrated votes convert efficiently while dispersed votes convert to nothing —
    the SNP's support was packed into Scottish seats, UKIP's spread everywhere and first
    nowhere. Seat share tracks concentration, not vote share. (2) The candidate who
    would beat every rival head-to-head; the sequential eliminations of IRV elected
    someone else, the textbook case that transferring votes moves paradoxes without
    abolishing them. (3) It asked a clear constitutional question in two stages — an
    indicative vote, then a binding one — and gave the redrawing to an independent
    commission, insulating rule-changing from incumbent self-interest.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Electoral systems are translation rules between votes and power; the rule shapes parties, campaigns and coalitions more than voters realise.</li>
    <li>FPTP is simple and decisive but wastes dispersed votes, invites spoilers, and trends two-party (Duverger's law).</li>
    <li>The PR families trade fairness for coalition politics: list systems are most proportional, STV keeps local representation, MMP hybridises both.</li>
    <li>Majoritarian fixes (IRV, runoffs) manufacture majorities and kill simple spoilers, but Burlington 2009 shows paradoxes moving rather than dying.</li>
    <li>Arrow's theorem and Gibbard–Satterthwaite are proofs, not opinions: every system with 3+ options carries unfairness — design chooses which.</li>
    <li>Referendums suit constitutional and cross-cutting moral questions, but binary framing, wording effects and turnout selection are engineered risks.</li>
    <li>Districting and even ballot-paper design are measurable parameters: the efficiency gap, ensemble simulations, order effects — each can be a kingmaker on close margins.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Constitutions and Rights</em> takes up the minority safeguards and judicial
    review that bound the referendum discussion, and <em>The Public Policy Cycle</em>
    follows what elected governments — whoever the translation rule seats — actually do
    once in office. Both sibling lessons assume the vocabulary you now have.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Where systems come from.</strong> The comparative record punctures the
    assumption that systems are chosen on the merits: <strong>incumbents choose
    electoral systems</strong> (Colomer's dictum) — usually ones that served the
    moment's winners (New Zealand's FPTP was chosen by parties it later failed;
    its reform came only when FPTP kept producing "wrong" winners). Analysis must
    therefore be political, not just technical: any reform proposal implies a
    coalition question — who loses the current translation rule badly enough to
    force change, and can they hold power long enough to change it?
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Lijphart's map.</strong> The <em>Patterns of Democracy</em> framework
    sorts democracies along a <strong>majoritarian-consensual</strong> dimension
    (disproportionate, two-party, concentrated power vs proportional, multiparty,
    power-sharing) and finds both clusters democratic and stable, differing
    reliably in how they treat minorities and opposition — the deepest empirical
    defence of treating system choice as a values question, not an optimisation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Does ranked-choice voting actually reduce
    polarisation (early US city evidence is encouraging but thin)? Can internet
    voting ever be made as verifiable as paper (the cryptography community's
    near-consensus: not yet, maybe not ever — election integrity needs
    universally checkable records)? Will algorithmic redistricting — optimise for
    compactness and communities, not partisan gain — become a neutral referee, or
    a new battlefield? And as algorithms enter campaigning (micro-targeted
    persuasion), does the <em>campaign</em> side need the design scrutiny the
    <em>counting</em> side got? The discipline's original insight still runs the
    show: rules shape outcomes — which means rule-writers are always in the game,
    and the referee question never closes.
  </p>
